1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,899
This is Sentinel: an AI-powered security
app for Windows and Mac that works

2
00:00:04,939 --> 00:00:06,150
completely offline.

3
00:00:06,647 --> 00:00:10,403
In the next few minutes, I'll show you how
it finds open ports,

4
00:00:10,443 --> 00:00:13,115
spots apps talking to servers they
shouldn't,

5
00:00:13,155 --> 00:00:15,887
and fixes vulnerabilities with a single
click.

6
00:00:17,279 --> 00:00:19,510
Everything starts on the dashboard.

7
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:23,273
One scan checks your open ports, your
network connections,

8
00:00:23,313 --> 00:00:25,609
and your system's security settings.

9
00:00:26,101 --> 00:00:29,313
On the left are the most exposed ports,
ranked by risk,

10
00:00:29,353 --> 00:00:32,151
along with the application that opened
each one.

11
00:00:32,642 --> 00:00:35,325
In the middle is your overall security
score.

12
00:00:35,365 --> 00:00:38,592
This machine scores poorly, with four
critical issues.

13
00:00:39,083 --> 00:00:43,209
On the right, the vulnerability board
counts open issues by severity,

14
00:00:43,249 --> 00:00:47,013
including two that attackers are actively
exploiting right now.

15
00:00:47,505 --> 00:00:50,491
Below that, you can see how exposed your
ports are,

16
00:00:50,531 --> 00:00:52,983
what Sentinel recommends doing about them,

17
00:00:53,023 --> 00:00:55,535
and the state of every outbound
connection.

18
00:00:56,927 --> 00:00:59,081
Let's look at the open ports.

19
00:00:59,121 --> 00:01:05,133
Each row shows the port, the service, and
the exact program behind it: its name,

20
00:01:05,173 --> 00:01:07,857
process ID, user, and file location.

21
00:01:08,348 --> 00:01:12,608
Click any row, and Sentinel's AI explains
the risk in plain English.

22
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:14,668
This one is a red flag.

23
00:01:14,708 --> 00:01:18,724
Port 4444, the default port for Metasploit
reverse shells,

24
00:01:18,764 --> 00:01:22,360
opened by a program running from a
temporary folder.

25
00:01:22,853 --> 00:01:24,838
To close it, click "Close port".

26
00:01:24,878 --> 00:01:27,622
Sentinel offers to block it in the
firewall,

27
00:01:27,662 --> 00:01:30,343
and it can also stop the program behind
it.

28
00:01:30,833 --> 00:01:32,781
I'll choose "Yes, close port".

29
00:01:32,821 --> 00:01:37,883
Windows asks for administrator permission,
and the port is blocked instantly.

30
00:01:38,378 --> 00:01:42,658
Blocked ports are marked with a lock, and
you can undo any block with one click.

31
00:01:44,051 --> 00:01:45,814
Next, the network monitor.

32
00:01:45,854 --> 00:01:51,155
Every fifteen seconds, Sentinel checks
which applications are connecting out,

33
00:01:51,195 --> 00:01:52,611
and exactly where to.

34
00:01:53,101 --> 00:01:55,962
Here it caught a program called
update.exe,

35
00:01:56,002 --> 00:01:59,200
sending traffic to a cryptocurrency mining
pool.

36
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:01,831
That's a classic sign of cryptojacking.

37
00:02:02,323 --> 00:02:07,516
And this file, opened from the Downloads
folder, is talking to a raw IP address,

38
00:02:07,556 --> 00:02:10,133
on a port used by remote-access trojans.

39
00:02:10,624 --> 00:02:13,560
Sentinel also learns what's normal for
each app.

40
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:18,024
After the first day, any brand-new
destination is flagged as an anomaly.

41
00:02:18,516 --> 00:02:21,373
For strict control, switch to Allowlist
mode.

42
00:02:21,413 --> 00:02:25,816
Then any destination you haven't approved
is flagged as unauthorised.

43
00:02:26,307 --> 00:02:29,037
One click blocks the address in the
firewall.

44
00:02:29,077 --> 00:02:33,037
And if you don't recognise the program,
Sentinel can stop it too.

45
00:02:36,124 --> 00:02:37,446
Now, vulnerabilities.

46
00:02:37,486 --> 00:02:42,569
Sentinel audits your operating system, and
checks your installed apps against a

47
00:02:42,609 --> 00:02:44,904
built-in database of known exploits.

48
00:02:45,396 --> 00:02:48,893
Findings are grouped by category, and
sorted by severity.

49
00:02:48,933 --> 00:02:52,306
Anything being exploited in the wild is
clearly marked.

50
00:02:52,797 --> 00:02:55,318
Click "How to fix", and you get a full

51
00:02:55,358 --> 00:02:58,257
playbook: why it matters, the manual
steps,

52
00:02:58,297 --> 00:03:01,007
and the exact commands Sentinel will run.

53
00:03:01,944 --> 00:03:04,091
Click "Apply fix automatically".

54
00:03:04,131 --> 00:03:09,694
Sentinel applies the fix, then re-checks
the system, to confirm it's really fixed.

55
00:03:10,185 --> 00:03:15,402
For missing patches, run a deep scan, and
Sentinel lists every update that Windows

56
00:03:15,442 --> 00:03:17,325
or macOS hasn't installed yet.

57
00:03:18,718 --> 00:03:20,614
All of this feeds the AI advisor.

58
00:03:20,654 --> 00:03:23,078
You can ask it anything about your system.

59
00:03:23,995 --> 00:03:28,541
It answers using your live scan results
and its built-in security knowledge.

60
00:03:28,581 --> 00:03:31,015
No internet, and no cloud model required.

61
00:03:31,506 --> 00:03:35,554
On the right is your action plan, ordered
by impact versus effort,

62
00:03:35,594 --> 00:03:37,846
so you always know what to fix first.

63
00:03:38,338 --> 00:03:43,512
After fixing things, press "Refresh", and
anything that's resolved drops off the

64
00:03:43,552 --> 00:03:43,838
plan.

65
00:03:44,329 --> 00:03:47,277
If you have a local AI model installed
with Ollama,

66
00:03:47,317 --> 00:03:50,324
Sentinel can use it for more
conversational answers.

67
00:03:50,364 --> 00:03:52,199
But everything works without it.

68
00:03:53,591 --> 00:03:54,661
Finally, settings.

69
00:03:54,701 --> 00:03:57,682
Here you manage your allowed and blocked
domains,

70
00:03:57,722 --> 00:04:01,751
and import extra threat-intelligence feeds
whenever you're online.

71
00:04:02,563 --> 00:04:05,051
Every firewall rule Sentinel creates is

72
00:04:05,091 --> 00:04:07,943
listed here, so you can undo it at any
time.

73
00:04:07,983 --> 00:04:10,653
And the activity log records every action.

74
00:04:12,046 --> 00:04:13,408
And that's Sentinel.

75
00:04:13,448 --> 00:04:17,684
Your ports, your network, and your
patches, all in one place,

76
00:04:17,724 --> 00:04:19,296
and completely offline.

77
00:04:19,786 --> 00:04:23,342
If you found this video useful, please
like, subscribe,

78
00:04:23,382 --> 00:04:26,676
and share it with someone who cares about
security.

79
00:04:27,167 --> 00:04:30,274
And if you'd like a copy of Sentinel for
yourself,

80
00:04:30,314 --> 00:04:32,728
just send me a message here on YouTube.

81
00:04:32,768 --> 00:04:33,987
Thanks for watching.

